WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.737
This short chapter covers one rule of thumb, forty fifty sixty, seventy eighty ninety, which links a pulse oximeter reading to the arterial PaO2.

2
00:00:12.737 --> 00:00:18.897
It lets you estimate the PaO2 at the bedside without drawing a blood gas.

3
00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:35.100
We cover how to use it, why the sixty ninety pair matters most, because it marks hypoxemia and the need for oxygen, and the limits of the rule, including oximeter accuracy and carbon monoxide.

4
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:46.788
PaO2 values of forty, fifty and sixty torr are about equal to saturations of seventy, eighty and ninety percent.

5
00:00:46.788 --> 00:00:52.116
Use it to estimate the PaO2 from a pulse oximeter reading.

6
00:00:52.116 --> 00:01:06.905
RCP 203 Lesson 1 adds that the rule applies when the saturation is below ninety percent, and it is approximate; the decks say about equal, or roughly correspond.

7
00:01:06.905 --> 00:01:14.253
For example, a saturation of eighty percent suggests a PaO2 of about fifty torr.

8
00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:22.245
Hess never names the rule, but describes the S-shaped oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve.

9
00:01:22.245 --> 00:01:36.942
At very low PO2 the curve is flat, then its slope rises rapidly, and at about seventy the curve flattens again, with saturation approaching one hundred percent.

10
00:01:36.942 --> 00:01:40.800
That steep middle is where the rule lives.

11
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.950
Pause and estimate.

12
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:50.399
A patient on room air has a pulse oximeter reading of seventy percent.

13
00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:57.400
Using the rule, what is the approximate PaO2, and is this patient hypoxemic?

14
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:04.300
[6-second practice pause]

15
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:11.263
A saturation of seventy percent suggests a PaO2 of about forty torr.

16
00:02:11.263 --> 00:02:19.247
That is well below sixty, so the patient is hypoxemic and needs supplemental oxygen.

17
00:02:19.247 --> 00:02:26.375
Remember the rule is only approximate, and it applies below ninety percent.

18
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.007
The top pair is the oxygen therapy threshold.

19
00:02:32.007 --> 00:02:44.722
Hypoxemia is a PaO2 below sixty torr, or a saturation below ninety percent, on room air, and chapter eleven says below fifty in infants.

20
00:02:44.722 --> 00:02:52.389
A saturation below ninety on any FiO2 means the patient needs supplemental oxygen.

21
00:02:52.389 --> 00:03:01.925
Weaning wants a PaO2 above sixty, or a saturation above ninety, on an FiO2 of point four zero or less.

22
00:03:01.925 --> 00:03:15.108
Near the eighty fifty pair, central cyanosis first appears: a saturation below eighty, a PaO2 of forty five to fifty, with normal hemoglobin.

23
00:03:15.108 --> 00:03:29.225
Normal saturation is above ninety three to ninety five percent on room air, and Lesson 1 gives an adult reference range of ninety five to ninety eight.

24
00:03:30.633 --> 00:03:35.720
Chapter eleven's oxygen targets are separate from the rule.

25
00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:44.773
Healthy patients aim for a PaO2 of eighty to one hundred with a saturation of ninety two percent or more.

26
00:03:44.773 --> 00:04:01.240
If that needs fifty percent oxygen or more, accept a PaO2 of at least fifty five to sixty with a saturation of eighty eight or more, and in chronic hypercapnia, a PaO2 of fifty five to sixty.

27
00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.965
The rule has limits.

28
00:04:02.965 --> 00:04:11.328
Pulse oximeters are accurate to only plus or minus two to four percent, even with good perfusion.

29
00:04:11.328 --> 00:04:22.708
They overestimate the saturation when carboxyhemoglobin is present, and pulse oximetry is not accurate in carbon monoxide poisoning.

30
00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:24.945
One more.

31
00:04:24.945 --> 00:04:31.256
A smoke inhalation patient's pulse oximeter reads ninety four percent.

32
00:04:31.256 --> 00:04:38.558
Can you use the rule, or the reading itself, to judge this patient's oxygenation?

33
00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:45.467
[6-second practice pause]

34
00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:46.567
No.

35
00:04:46.567 --> 00:04:57.391
After smoke inhalation, carboxyhemoglobin may be present, and the pulse oximeter overestimates saturation when it is.

36
00:04:57.391 --> 00:05:07.753
Pulse oximetry is not accurate in carbon monoxide poisoning, so neither the reading nor the rule can be trusted.

37
00:05:07.753 --> 00:05:12.749
And the rule only applies below ninety percent anyway.

38
00:05:12.749 --> 00:05:15.942
Check the saturation with CO-oximetry.

39
00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:20.081
Here is the chapter in brief.

40
00:05:20.081 --> 00:05:32.622
A PaO2 of forty, fifty and sixty roughly equals a saturation of seventy, eighty and ninety percent, and the rule applies below ninety.

41
00:05:32.622 --> 00:05:39.361
Sixty and ninety is the hypoxemia line and the oxygen therapy threshold.

42
00:05:39.361 --> 00:05:43.760
Central cyanosis appears near eighty and fifty.

43
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:50.031
Normal saturation is above ninety three to ninety five on room air.

44
00:05:50.031 --> 00:06:02.292
And the rule is only as good as the oximeter, which is accurate to two to four percent and unreliable in carbon monoxide poisoning.
